TIPS = [
("serve-deep", "Serve", "Serve deep, not hard",
 "A serve landing near the baseline pushes the receiver back and buys you time. Depth matters more than pace.",
 "<p>You only get one serve. Missing it hands over the serve. So aim for a big zone about three feet inside the opponent's baseline rather than for the lines.</p><p>A deep serve forces the receiver back. Their return will be shorter, and your third shot easier.</p>",
 "Put a towel three feet inside the far baseline. Ten serves: how many land beyond it?", "2026-11-27"),
("return-and-move-up", "Return", "After the return, get to the kitchen",
 "The receiving team has an edge: it can move to the net right after the return. Don't waste it by staying back.",
 "<p>Thanks to the two-bounce rule, the server must let your return bounce. Use that time to move forward. Hit your return deep and slow: it gives you more time to reach the kitchen line.</p><p>Stop just behind the line, feet apart, paddle in front.</p>",
 "Hit ten returns and count your steps to the kitchen line out loud. Goal: be there before the ball comes back over the net.", "2026-11-27"),
("third-shot-drop", "Strategy", "The third shot is the drop",
 "The serving team is stuck at the back. A drop, a soft ball into the opponent's kitchen, lets it move up without being punished.",
 "<p>Drive your third shot at opponents already at the net and it'll come back at your feet. The drop forces them to play a low ball they can't attack.</p><p>Aim just over the net with a soft arc, and take two or three steps forward as soon as it leaves your paddle.</p>",
 "From the baseline, hit twenty drops into the opponent's kitchen. Count how many bounce there.", "2026-11-27"),
("paddle-up", "Technique", "Paddle up, always",
 "At the net, exchanges are too fast to bring your paddle up from your waist. Keep it in front, at chest height.",
 "<p>It's the number one beginner habit: the paddle drops after every shot. So you're always late on speed-ups.</p><p>Ready position: paddle in front of your chest, head slightly up, elbows in front of your body. Come back to it after every shot.</p>",
 "During a game, ask your partner to shout \"paddle\" every time yours drops below your waist.", "2026-11-27"),
("dink-patience", "Strategy", "Dinking is patience",
 "A dink rally is rarely won by a brilliant shot. It's won when the other side loses patience and pops the ball up.",
 "<p>The point of a dink isn't to win the rally but to give your opponent a ball they can't attack: low, in the kitchen, ideally to their backhand.</p><p>Wait for the ball that's too high. That one you can speed up.</p>",
 "Dink rally with a partner: first to hit outside the kitchen or into the net loses the point. Play to 11.", "2026-11-27"),
("doubles-talk", "Doubles", "Three words that save a doubles game",
 "\"Mine\", \"yours\", \"leave it\". In doubles, half the points lost down the middle come from silence.",
 "<p>The ball down the middle is the most dangerous: each player thinks the other has it. Agree before the match. Often, the player with the forehand in the middle takes it.</p><p>And shout \"leave it\" when a ball is going out. Your partner doesn't always have time to judge.</p>",
 "Play a game where every middle ball must be called out loud. Fault if nobody speaks.", "2026-11-27"),
("kitchen-momentum", "Rules", "Watch your momentum after a volley",
 "Volley just in front of the line, then step into the kitchen, even after the rally ends: that's a fault. Momentum counts.",
 "<p>It's the most frustrating fault: the winning volley, then the foot sliding into the kitchen. The French federation tightened this rule in September 2026.</p><p>Stay slightly back from the line to volley, and brake before you hit, not after.</p>",
 "Volley with a partner and freeze after each shot. Feet still before the next contact.", "2026-11-27"),
("aim-at-feet", "Strategy", "Aim at the feet, not the head",
 "A ball arriving at a net player's feet is almost impossible to return aggressively.",
 "<p>When you speed up, the temptation is to hit at chest height. But a high ball is easy to volley. A low ball at the shoes forces a defensive half-volley.</p>",
 "Lay a paddle on the ground in front of your partner's feet and aim at it for ten speed-ups.", "2026-11-27"),
("warm-up", "Health", "Five minutes of warm-up",
 "Pickleball injuries mostly hit ankles, calves and shoulders. Five minutes are enough to prevent most of them.",
 "<p>Before playing: brisk walking, high knees, side shuffles, shoulder and wrist rotations. Then a few easy dinks before speeding up.</p><p>Lateral movement and sudden stops are what load the body most.</p>",
 "Before your next session: 1 minute of side shuffles, 1 minute of shoulder rotations, 3 minutes of dinks.", "2026-11-27"),
("call-the-score", "Rules", "Call the score out loud",
 "In doubles, the score has three numbers and everyone gets lost. Calling it before each serve prevents arguments.",
 "<p>The server calls their score, then the opponents', then their server number: \"4-2-1\". The game starts at \"0-0-2\".</p><p>Tip: when your team's score is even, your team's first server should be on the right. If not, something's wrong.</p>",
 "For a whole game, each player calls the score when it's their turn to serve.", "2026-11-27"),
("dink-to-backhand", "Technique", "Dink to the backhand",
 "For most players the backhand is weaker, especially at the net. Send your dinks there.",
 "<p>Spot who's right- and left-handed during the warm-up. In doubles, the diagonal to a right-hander's backhand is often the most comfortable to attack.</p>",
 "During a dink rally, play only to your partner's backhand. They must keep the ball low.", "2026-11-27"),
("lob-sparingly", "Strategy", "Lob, but rarely",
 "A good lob is beautiful. A short lob is a gift-wrapped smash. Keep it as a surprise.",
 "<p>The lob works against opponents glued to the kitchen line and leaning forward. It fails in wind or when the opponent is already backing up.</p><p>Outdoors with the wind behind you, forget it.</p>",
 "Hit five lobs in practice and note how many truly land behind the opponent's service line.", "2026-11-27"),
("reset", "Technique", "Learn the reset",
 "Under pressure, don't counterattack: slow down. A soft ball into the kitchen puts everyone back on equal terms.",
 "<p>When an opponent speeds up at you mid-court, the best answer is often a soft ball, open paddle, dying in the kitchen. That's a reset.</p><p>The secret: a loose grip and a short motion that absorbs pace instead of returning it.</p>",
 "Your partner at the net speeds up at you from the kitchen. You, mid-court, send every ball back into their kitchen.", "2026-11-27"),
("indoor-outdoor-balls", "Gear", "Use the right ball",
 "Outdoor ball indoors: it bounces too much and skids. Indoor ball outdoors: the wind takes it.",
 "<p>Outdoor balls are heavier and harder, with more, smaller holes. Indoor balls are lighter and softer. They have 26 to 40 holes depending on the model.</p><p>In sanctioned play, the ball must be on the USA Pickleball approved list.</p>",
 "If you play in both conditions, keep a tube of each in your bag and label which is which.", "2026-11-27"),
("continental-grip", "Technique", "One grip for everything",
 "The \"hammer\", or continental, grip lets you play forehand, backhand and volley without changing grip.",
 "<p>Hold the paddle like a hammer, edge towards the net. At the net, you'll never have time to switch grips between volleys.</p><p>Don't squeeze: on a scale of 1 to 10, stay around 4.</p>",
 "Play a whole dink session holding the hammer grip, never adjusting it.", "2026-11-27"),
("transition-zone", "Strategy", "Don't stay mid-court",
 "The area between the baseline and the kitchen, the transition zone, is where balls land at your feet. Move through it.",
 "<p>After the third shot, move up in stages: hit, step in, split-step as your opponent hits, then go again. Aim to reach the kitchen line in two or three shots.</p>",
 "From the baseline, try to reach the kitchen in three shots max while your partner feeds you low balls.", "2026-11-27"),
("move-together", "Doubles", "Move as if you're tied together",
 "In doubles, picture a ten-foot rope between you and your partner. When one moves, the other follows.",
 "<p>If your partner is pulled wide, slide to the middle to close the gap. If you move up, move up together. A staggered team, one up and one back, opens a highway down the diagonal.</p>",
 "Play a game where you must keep your partner beside you, never in front or behind.", "2026-11-27"),
("speed-up", "Strategy", "Speed up the right ball",
 "A speed-up at the wrong moment comes back faster than it left. Wait for a ball above the net.",
 "<p>In a dink rally, don't speed up a low ball: you'd have to hit upwards, and your opponent just volleys down.</p><p>The right ball to speed up arrives at hip height or higher.</p>",
 "During a dink rally, only speed up balls that clear the top of the net by more than eight inches.", "2026-11-27"),
("play-the-wind", "Outdoors", "Play the wind",
 "Wind behind you: play shorter and softer. Wind in your face: you can hit harder, the ball will be slowed.",
 "<p>The pickleball is light and perforated: wind carries it far more than a tennis ball. At every change of ends, recalibrate.</p>",
 "At the warm-up, hit five dinks each way to feel the wind before the first point.", "2026-11-27"),
("one-point", "Mindset", "One point at a time",
 "Only the serving team scores: you can lose six rallies without losing a point. Don't panic.",
 "<p>Pickleball's scoring system is your friend in tough moments. As long as your opponent doesn't serve well, they don't score. Focus on the current rally, not the score.</p>",
 "After each lost point, take one slow breath before resetting. Three seconds, no more.", "2026-11-27"),
("erne", "Advanced", "The erne, for bold players",
 "Going around the kitchen to volley from outside the court is legal, as long as you don't touch the zone.",
 "<p>When an opponent dinks along the sideline, you can jump over the corner of the kitchen, or set up outside the court beside it, to volley. Surprise guaranteed.</p><p>Condition: your feet must not touch the kitchen or its lines, before or during the shot.</p>",
 "In practice, walk slowly past the kitchen five times without a ball to memorise where to put your feet.", "2026-11-27"),
("light-paddle", "Gear", "Beginner? Go light",
 "A 7 to 8 oz paddle forgives mistakes and protects your elbow. You can move to heavier later.",
 "<p>Heavy paddles add power, but in pickleball control wins points. And a tired arm misses dinks.</p><p>Expect €45 to €85 for a good first paddle.</p>",
 "Before buying, test two paddles of different weights at your club, at least twenty minutes each.", "2026-11-27"),
("aim-middle", "Doubles", "When in doubt, go middle",
 "A ball between the two opponents creates hesitation and cuts the angles they can send back.",
 "<p>Aiming at the sidelines is risky: little margin, and the opponent can reply with an angle. The middle is safe, and it's often where confusion starts on the other side.</p>",
 "For one game, hit every attacking ball down the middle. Count the errors it causes.", "2026-11-27"),
("shoes-first", "Health", "Shoes before the paddle",
 "Pickleball means lateral movement and sudden stops. Running shoes are a sprain waiting to happen.",
 "<p>Get court shoes: tennis or padel outdoors, indoor court shoes inside. Good ankle support and a sole that grips without sticking.</p>",
 "Check your current soles: if they're smooth or heavily rounded at the sides, replace them before your next session.", "2026-11-27"),
]
